      "reason": "test_problem: The PRP's decision C4 says no existing test pins hashlib.sha1 identity. That's wrong: tests/test_itsdangerous/test_serializer.py:180 (TestSerializer::test_iter_unsigners) asserts `next(unsigners).digest_method == hashlib.sha1` for a signer built with the default digest. I implemented the PRP as written: a module-level `_lazy_sha1` that calls `hashlib.sha1(string)` in its body, with both `default_digest_method` attributes set to `staticmethod(_lazy_sha1)`, the docstrings updated and a CHANGES.rst bullet added. My new subprocess tests went from failing to passing. But that test then fails in all 10 of its parametrized cases, because `_lazy_sha1 != hashlib.sha1`. Its sibling test_digests (line 186) still passes, since the signature bytes don't change. The PRP requires the full existing suite to keep passing and doesn't allow editing existing tests. The only way to keep the assertion true without editing it would be a hack, such as making `_lazy_sha1` an object with a custom `__eq__` that compares equal to hashlib.sha1. That goes against the PRP's choice of a plain function and isn't a real fix. A human needs to decide whether the PRP may allow changing test_serializer.py:180 to compare against `itsdangerous.signer._lazy_sha1`, or to check behaviour instead, e.g. `digest_method(b\"x\").digest() == hashlib.sha1(b\"x\").digest()`. The upstream fix for this issue made that change. I reverted my changes, so the repo is unchanged.",
